Let $(\#,\eta)$ be a triangulated duality with exactness sign $\delta\in\{1,-1\}$ on a category in which multiplication by $2$ is invertible on every morphism group. Let $u:L\to\#L$ be symmetric, without an invertibility assumption. In a triangle $$L\xrightarrow{u}\#L\xrightarrow{v}C\xrightarrow{w}L[1],$$ the cone has a nonsingular symmetric form $\psi:C\xrightarrow{\sim}[1]\#C$ for the translated duality. It is neutral and can be chosen to satisfy $$\label{cat:cone-compatibility}
 \psi v=-[1](\#w),
 \qquad [1](\#v)\psi=\delta[1](\eta_L)w,$$ with the dual shift comparisons understood. For $\#=\mathcal D_{X,r+1}$ this is a neutral form of degree $r$.

For $A\in\mathcal T(X)$ and $B\in\mathcal C_I$, the external product $B\boxtimes A$ belongs to $\mathcal T(I\times X)$; the same assertion holds for $\mathcal P$. For all integers $s,r$, the natural map $$\label{cat:external-duality}
 \mu_{s,r}:\mathcal D_{I,s}B\boxtimes\mathcal D_{X,r}A
 \xrightarrow{\sim}
 \mathcal D_{I\times X,s+r}(B\boxtimes A)$$ is an isomorphism. The map is adjoint to the product of evaluations, with the tensor shift signs; it commutes with proper direct image, duality, and the trace maps. In particular the product of symmetric pairings is symmetric for the sum of their degrees.

If $X=U\cup V$ with $U,V$ open, extension by zero induces a fully faithful exact functor preserving duality $$\label{loc:eq-open-cover}
 \mathcal B(V)/\mathcal B(U\cap V)
 \longrightarrow\mathcal B(X)/\mathcal B(U).$$ Its replete image is dense: every object of the target is an actual summand of an object in the image. These functors commute with $\mathcal P\to\mathcal T$.

Let $d\geq3$ be odd, with orientation class $u_d$ as above. There is a positive power of $2$, denoted by $L_d$, such that $$\label{lat:lattice}
 \begin{gathered}
 E_d(S^d)\otimes\mathbb Q=\mathbb Q\overline{u}_d,\\
 \mathop{\mathrm{im}}\bigl(E_d(S^d)\to E_d(S^d)\otimes\mathbb Q\bigr)
 \subseteq L_d^{-1}\mathbb Z\overline{u}_d.
 \end{gathered}$$ One may take $L_d=2^{e_d}$. This denominator is fixed by the sphere dimension and comparison. It is independent of every later action, test map, character partition, prime $p$, and exponent $k$.

Let $d\geq3$ be odd. Let $X$ be any test space for $\mathcal T$, and let $U\subset X$ be a nonempty contractible open subset. Then the quotient functor induces an integral isomorphism $$\label{lat:contractible-isomorphism}
 E_d(X)\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }
 W_d\bigl(\mathcal T(X)/\mathcal T(U)\bigr).$$ In particular this applies to compact test polyhedra and to their manifold-with-boundary models. It is natural for continuous maps $g:X\to X'$ with $g(U)\subset U'$, whenever $U'$ is also a nonempty contractible open subset.

There are canonical isomorphisms $$\label{char:orbit-sum}
 Rq_*\underline{\mathbb C}_{W^+}\simeq\mathcal H
       \simeq\bigoplus_{\lambda\in\Lambda}\mathcal H_\lambda.$$ For every continuous map $t:Y\to X$ to a compact Hausdorff space and every $D\subset\Lambda$, the natural map $$\label{char:derived-sum}
 \bigoplus_{\lambda\in D}Rt_*\mathcal H_\lambda
       \longrightarrow Rt_*\mathcal H_D$$ is an isomorphism in the bounded-below derived category of real sheaves on $X$. The sum on the left is a coproduct in the ambient derived category.

Let $B\in\mathcal C_I$, $A\in\mathcal T(X)$ (or the corresponding semialgebraic category), and $s,r\in\mathbb Z$. Write $$\gamma_s(B)=[-s]\kappa_1:
 \mathcal D_{I,s}(B[1])\longrightarrow(\mathcal D_{I,s}B)[-1].$$ Use the first-factor shift comparisons $$\begin{aligned}
 \sigma: B[1]\boxtimes A&\xrightarrow{\sim}(B\boxtimes A)[1],\\
 \epsilon: (\mathcal D_{I,s}B)[-1]\boxtimes\mathcal D_{X,r}A
 &\xrightarrow{\sim}(\mathcal D_{I,s}B\boxtimes\mathcal D_{X,r}A)[-1].
 \end{aligned}$$ They introduce no sign under <a href="#cat:tensor-output-shifts" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="cat:tensor-output-shifts">[cat:tensor-output-shifts]</a>. Indicate the inputs of the product comparison by superscripts and put $$\widetilde\mu_{s,r}
   =(\mathcal D_{I\times X,s+r}\sigma)^{-1}\mu_{s,r}^{B[1],A},
 \qquad
 \lambda=\epsilon\circ(\gamma_s(B)\boxtimes1).$$ Then the two routes to the suspended total dual satisfy $$\label{cat:suspension-comparison}
 \gamma_{s+r}(B\boxtimes A)\,\widetilde\mu_{s,r}
   =(-1)^r\mu_{s,r}^{B,A}[-1]\,\lambda.$$ The scalar $(-1)^r$ is independent of $A$, its cohomological degrees, and the point of $I$.
